.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_ALLOCATED_BYTES 3 "2026-09-16" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes \- report the memory a ranged pool holds for its entries
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes(ccol_r_mempool *rmp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the sizes of the entry blocks of every tier added together, in
bytes. That total is what a ranged pool costs.

Each tier is a pool in its own right, with its own element size and
element count, and each tier large enough to keep a cache for each
thread holds a reserve of its own, so this total exceeds the sum of the
tier capacities. Use this figure when you size a ranged pool against a
memory budget, which is where a computation by hand is hardest.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp                       Ranged memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of the entry block of every tier, added
together
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The call counts the entry blocks of the tiers and nothing else, on the
same terms as ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(3). The handles, the cache of
each thread, and every entry that a fallback policy serves all stay
outside this figure.

The value does not change over the lifetime of the pool.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the lock of each tier in turn, which makes it a
cold-path call: do not use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create (3)
